# thermiagenesis2mqtt

Exposes a Thermia Genesis platform heat pump (Mega, Diplomat, ...) to MQTT following the
[Homie 5 convention](https://homieiot.github.io/). Talks to the heat pump over Modbus TCP.

Register definitions come from the Thermia "Modbus protocol for Mega, Genesis platform" user guide,
version 12.00.

## Usage

```
thermiagenesis2mqtt mqtt --tcp heatpump.local --mqtt-broker mqtt://broker:1883?client_id=thermia
thermiagenesis2mqtt registers "tap water"
```

By default **nothing is ever written** to the heat pump: all the properties are read-only and no
`/set` topics are subscribed to. Pass `--allow-writes` to be able to change the settings (such as
the tap water start and stop temperatures) from MQTT.

`THERMIAGENESIS2MQTT_LOG=debug` makes it more talkative. At startup it logs how many
requests one polling cycle takes. The heat pump takes about half a second to answer any request. Each
connection to it waits for the response before sending the next request, but the connections are
served independently, so `--connections` (4 by default) is what makes polling fast. `--max-read-count`
makes for fewer, larger requests.

Properties that have no value (for example a sensor that is not installed, which the heat pump
reports as 200 °C) are left out of the `$description`, as a Homie device that is `ready` has to have
a value for everything it describes. When that changes while running the device goes through
`$state = init` and publishes the new description.

Lists (active alarms, running demands, the heat curve) are published as JSON and the operating times
as ISO 8601 durations.

## Design

There are two layers.

* `modbus` talks to the heat pump. It polls the registers that are of interest (grouped by how often
  they should be polled, and read out in as few requests as possible), keeps a snapshot of their
  contents and sends a single notification for each completed read out transaction that changed
  something. It knows nothing about what the registers mean.
* `homie` knows what the registers mean. A node is a group of properties, and a property computes
  its value out of the snapshot. It does not map to registers 1:1: a software version is three
  registers, an operating hours counter is two, the list of active alarms is some 80 discrete
  inputs. When anything changes all the properties depending on the changed registers recompute
  their values and the ones that came out different get published.

`registers` has the table of registers (generated by `tools/gen-registers.awk` from the output of
`pdftotext -layout` on the guide) and the types to refer to them.

The nodes that are enabled by default are the ones that every installation has. Others (mixing
valve 1, electric meter) need accessories and can be enabled with `--nodes`.

## Trying it without a heat pump

`cargo run --example fake_heat_pump` is a stand-in that can only be read from, and
`cargo run --example mqtt_dump -- localhost:1883 <device name> <seconds> [--clear]` shows (and with
`--clear` erases) what a device has published.

## Caveats

* The guide does not say which registers are signed. Temperatures and a few others are treated as
  signed (see `is_signed` in the generator.)
* Not every heat pump has every register. If the heat pump rejects a request a warning is logged
  and the registers are left unpublished, which, as per the Homie convention, holds up the device
  from becoming ready.
